.TH IDE 4 "February 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
ide \- ATA/ATAPI IDE device driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <drivers/storage/ide/ide.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B ide
driver provides access to ATA (Parallel ATA) and ATAPI (CD-ROM/DVD)
devices attached to standard IDE controllers.
It supports both legacy PIO (Programmed I/O) and Bus Master DMA transfer
modes on ISA compatibility-mode controllers and PCI IDE controllers.

.SS Device Nodes
IDE devices are typically accessed via the block device interface under
.BR /dev/storage/ :
.TP
.B /dev/storage/ideN
The block device node for the \fIN\fP-th IDE drive.
Slot numbering is fixed by channel and drive:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide0\fP primary master
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide1\fP primary slave
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide2\fP secondary master
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide3\fP secondary slave
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide4\fP tertiary master
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide5\fP tertiary slave
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide6\fP quaternary master
.IP \(bu 4
\fBide7\fP quaternary slave
.RE

.SS Features
.TP
.B PIO Transfers
The driver supports LBA28 and LBA48 addressing for PIO transfers.
.B Optimization:
Data transfers in PIO mode are optimized using the
.B rep insw
and
.B rep outsw
instructions (see
.BR io (9)
) to transfer data in 256-word (512-byte) chunks. This reduces CPU overhead compared to byte-by-byte or word-by-word loops, particularly in virtualized environments where instruction emulation is costly.
.TP
.B DMA Support
Bus Master DMA support exists in the driver for compatible controllers (for
example Intel PIIX4-class IDE), but PCI probe does not enable it
automatically.  The default attach path stays PIO-first so that PCI/native
channel discovery does not silently change the transfer engine used for root
filesystem I/O.
PIO paths remain polling-based, so classic non-PCI systems do not depend on
DMA or shared-IRQ support.
If a device repeatedly fails DMA reads or writes at runtime after DMA has been
enabled, the driver demotes that device to PIO and keeps the controller online
instead of re-entering the same failing DMA path indefinitely.
After repeated transfer failures, the driver grants at most one successful
channel-reset recovery window before it marks the device offline.
.TP
.B ATAPI
Packet commands (ATAPI) are supported for CD-ROM and DVD drives, allowing SCSI-like command execution over the IDE interface.

.SH KERNEL CONFIGURATION
The driver automatically probes for devices on the standard legacy I/O ports:
.IP \(bu 4
Primary: 0x1F0 (Control 0x3F6), IRQ 14
.IP \(bu 4
Secondary: 0x170 (Control 0x376), IRQ 15
.IP \(bu 4
Tertiary: 0x1E8 (Control 0x3EE), IRQ 11
.IP \(bu 4
Quaternary: 0x168 (Control 0x36E), IRQ 10

If PCI IDE native mode is present, channel I/O bases and IRQs may be
overridden from PCI BAR and interrupt-line configuration.
Controllers exposing additional channel pairs through separate PCI functions
are mapped onto the tertiary and quaternary channel slots.
Bus-master DMA BARs may be discovered from PCI configuration space without
being enabled for normal transfers.
If PCI is absent, or if additional IDE channels are not exposed through PCI,
the driver falls back to the legacy fixed I/O model above.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scsi (4),
.BR io (9)
